WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.820
<v Ethan>Welcome back to Practical Systems. I'm joined again by Priya Nair, who spends her days keeping large systems upright. Priya, good to have you back.</v>

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.320
<v Priya Nair>Good to be back, Ethan.</v>

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:18.060
<v Ethan>This episode is brought to you by Datadog — unified observability across logs, metrics, and traces. Start at datadog.com/podcast.</v>

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.820
<v Ethan>We opened the phones this week. Here's a question that came in from a listener out in the field.</v>

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.540
<v Caller>Hi Ethan — how do you tell alert fatigue from a real incident?</v>

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:36.480
<v Priya Nair>Great question. Alert fatigue is a signal-to-noise problem, not a discipline problem. If the team ignores pages, the pages are wrong, not the people.</v>

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.600
<v Ethan>So you'd tune the alerts before you tune the humans.</v>

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:47.280
<v Priya Nair>Every time. Measure how many pages led to action. Under half, and you have a noise problem — no amount of willpower fixes noise.</v>

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.900
<v Ethan>Before we wrap — thanks to the caller for that one. Priya, always a pleasure.</v>

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.400
<v Priya Nair>Anytime, Ethan.</v>

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.460
<v Ethan>That's it for Practical Systems. See you next week.</v>
